  "result": "I've implemented `slugify` in `src/strings.js` and added 11 tests for it to `test/strings.test.js`. `npm test` now passes all 13 tests (11 new, 2 existing).\n\nHow it works:\n- It lowercases the text, then splits accented letters into the base letter plus the accent mark and deletes the accent. That turns \"Tiếng Việt\" into \"tieng viet\" and \"Crème Brûlée\" into \"creme brulee\".\n- Splitting the accent off doesn't work for đ, so a small table maps it to `d` (Đ becomes đ when lowercased, so it's covered too).\n- Every run of characters that aren't a–z or 0–9 becomes one hyphen, and hyphens at the start and end are removed.\n- The result is cut to 50 characters, and any hyphen left at the end of the cut is removed. So a cut slug can be shorter than 50.\n\n**One addition you didn't ask for:** the same table also handles a few other letters that keep their accent when split, mapping ð→d, ø→o, ł→l, ß→ss, æ→ae, œ→oe, þ→th and ı→i. Without it they'd just turn into hyphens. If you'd rather not have it, the table is one line and easy to trim.\n\nNon-Latin scripts such as Chinese or Cyrillic aren't converted. They count as \"not a–z or 0–9\", so they become hyphens and can leave an empty slug, as your rules say.",
